#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124156 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "225b0bca-3520-58ab-bd36-0e3de337b604" :question/math-academy-id "e-3068" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,8)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{4x}y - 4x^2y,$ find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,1,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_y(2,1).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ at $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,1) & =\\frac{4(2)}{(1)}-4(2)^{2}(1) \\\\  & =8-16 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124157 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "70bcf628-cf1f-5da0-85b7-19d0f514ccd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3069" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x = y\\cos(xy) + \\cos y,$ find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z = f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x} \\Bigg|_{(1,\\pi)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x}$ at $(1,\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi (-1)+(-1) \\\\  & =-\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )}(x-1) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-\\pi -1)(x-1) \\\\ z+1 & =(-\\pi -1)x+\\pi +1 \\\\ z & =-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ has the equation $y=\\pi.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\pi  \\\\ z=-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124158 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "75c0deaf-38a7-5a81-afac-cae2978d52b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3070" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin{\\left(2 x + \\dfrac{\\pi y}{4} \\right)},$ find a tangent vector to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_x(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_x(x,y)$ at $(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0)+\\frac{\\pi (1)}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-1 & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x \\\\ z & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(0,1,1)$ has the equation $y=1.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find a tangent vector, we can find a normal vector to each plane and compute their cross product.\n\n- For the plane $y = 1,$ we write $0x + 1y + 0z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 0,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $z = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x +1,$ we write $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x + 0y + 1z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{\\pi}4,0,1 \\right\\rangle.$\n\nComputing the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{4},0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\pi /4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we've found a tangent vector, and this could be our final answer. However, just to make our answer a little nicer, we can multiply the vector by $4$ to yield a simpler, parallel vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot (i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k)=4\\,i+\\pi \\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9829bd35-dce6-543f-a40a-71578ce0965f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321954} {:answer/id #uuid "7746ea2b-6e18-524f-b9cc-4542b80bad2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321955} {:answer/id #uuid "779e5211-b5b7-5136-a9da-f5f7f40f9240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321956} {:answer/id #uuid "e05f54fa-df5e-5702-b9e2-4bf0c3be2f0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321957} {:answer/id #uuid "7c56a0dc-54e8-5f1a-903d-61ac8b42e52c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c56a0dc-54e8-5f1a-903d-61ac8b42e52c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321958} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dc65800-a9b6-58fe-99c5-342a293c07c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6336c497-86b6-5ccd-aae1-b53920cad5d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-132579" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+xy$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-1-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5eb4210-e017-5d7f-ad07-224de2c738a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321961} {:answer/id #uuid "fa856d7b-712d-5412-b0c6-7fec9c462cc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321962} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a266a7-3ca8-595b-bc5c-daf58cb3e427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321963} {:answer/id #uuid "70f216c2-ce4a-5150-bbc8-73b81bf7c204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321964} {:answer/id #uuid "6a56d749-783e-5b37-bbd4-ea8ffd9368dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5eb4210-e017-5d7f-ad07-224de2c738a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321961} :answer-field/id #uuid "97985362-699c-57d3-8ddd-a09dfbf957f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81835159-51bf-5250-ba2c-6356bf0ceaa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-132584" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Given that the point $P(1,-3,8)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}+y\\sqrt{x}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-3,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,-3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-3) & =\\frac{{1}^{2}+1}{{1}^{2}(-3)^{2}}+(-3)\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}-3(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "991042b2-3d27-5a0b-a6e2-49d17fca0d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321968} {:answer/id #uuid "fb86dcd4-62b0-54a2-818f-cde5b9bd4e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321969} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e823a8-b2dd-5432-a6dd-7787c3f4a712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321970} {:answer/id #uuid "97721005-5ca9-5036-b701-78c3eff0d71d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=2y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321971} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6a2638-2da6-56b4-a918-890205bbd536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0e823a8-b2dd-5432-a6dd-7787c3f4a712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321970} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0ade84a-e009-5373-9e20-c5f73c9de403" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "442ef4cc-2e2a-5f75-9134-aec36af10017" :question/math-academy-id "q-132589" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=e.$ Given that the point $P(e,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=e.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(e,2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(e,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(e,2) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(e,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =1(y-2) \\\\ z & =y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(e,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "382f32da-fa62-598a-8373-1b07a14e418e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=x+2\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321975} {:answer/id #uuid "86c20059-5dfa-5ce1-8fe9-2a4c9cb54c49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=2x-2\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321976} {:answer/id #uuid "a583929c-bb75-579d-95b1-bf46b4cd8f55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321977} {:answer/id #uuid "8234ef11-10ca-5149-9ad8-a86240cd9fe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=x-\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321978} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc76348-2713-5cee-ac94-bae3d3c969af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fc76348-2713-5cee-ac94-bae3d3c969af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321979} :answer-field/id #uuid "95677b3e-be0f-51c7-aace-c7b0e0552362" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "760c6872-f52d-53e8-b7b3-88a52e70f20e" :question/math-academy-id "q-132590" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{xy}{\\pi }$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})(2)}{\\pi } \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)}(x-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\ z-2\\pi  & =-2(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ z & =-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3795097-21ef-5069-87b3-fdaf4499687b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321982} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ec3124-2d80-5025-bc2d-f74559ffd22b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321983} {:answer/id #uuid "eecb499d-01e5-5d99-9b67-13734354e5d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,0,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321984} {:answer/id #uuid "b6753f10-77bc-5c44-8c29-30d9379f2cef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321985} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea7d46d-e3a5-557f-84fe-1637e2fcd61d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3795097-21ef-5069-87b3-fdaf4499687b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321982} :answer-field/id #uuid "70f31201-de1e-54e0-8303-6d62dd785868" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "55ea53d1-23d3-50b0-af4a-d002f4c94b27" :question/math-academy-id "q-132599" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={e}^{y-1}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,1) & ={e}^{1-1}+2(2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-1) \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,1) & ={f}_{x}(2,1)(x-2) \\\\ z-4 & =5(x-2) \\\\ z & =5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,1,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=5x-6$ (or $5x-z-6=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨5,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-5⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-5⟩=⟨1,0,5⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bde33115-b5ec-568b-b700-2ecc5bb38784" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122261} {:answer/id #uuid "d12b216a-9e2e-57e3-9454-23c33fd456d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122262} {:answer/id #uuid "1ff395c8-9356-503b-a7b9-b06acd6e991b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122263} {:answer/id #uuid "39e67ac9-e3c4-53db-a615-f95a9f55b58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122264} {:answer/id #uuid "60c44c1e-bf6a-54fa-a439-0cc39843ba68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60c44c1e-bf6a-54fa-a439-0cc39843ba68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122265} :answer-field/id #uuid "e784d29d-4fb8-57f1-a536-c279710d5670" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63eca657-711b-57df-bd85-bce2f11b802d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36216" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=3.$ Given that the point $P(2,3,9)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=6x-y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,3,9)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,3) & =6(2)-(3)=9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65d0729f-4778-51b8-a483-1909ab84edf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4y+8\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321989} {:answer/id #uuid "a39aaeb9-f462-5498-b89c-1c8c6a9c0619" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4y+8\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321990} {:answer/id #uuid "43a5c0a2-5e4e-52c9-a835-0d0ada2e39fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4x+8\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321991} {:answer/id #uuid "c28ea5e1-e812-56d9-a4c7-c4ac2e6be747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4y-8\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321992} {:answer/id #uuid "b8627a81-a6be-5f51-833d-e2dac9e41cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a39aaeb9-f462-5498-b89c-1c8c6a9c0619" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4y+8\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321990} :answer-field/id #uuid "040cceeb-77af-5045-8aef-ab24f611ebe2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33bf0daa-0427-5567-9a5c-6a79e37f5a37" :question/math-academy-id "q-36301" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,0,8)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+4y+x$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,0,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)} & =(-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+4(0)+(-2) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)}(y-0) \\\\ z-8 & =-4(y-0) \\\\ z & =-4y+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,0,8)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4y+8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4878389-0d6f-5500-8584-aa949ccb29ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321995} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1f2e8c-9096-55e8-aa66-babb50da9455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321996} {:answer/id #uuid "def5f028-b86a-508a-a959-32f84b331d5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,1,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321997} {:answer/id #uuid "8b1543de-fbee-57f0-840f-09b15cf5f206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321998} {:answer/id #uuid "0f796832-1cc1-509b-afb5-012ac545dbd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a1f2e8c-9096-55e8-aa66-babb50da9455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321996} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f2d675e-7e56-5e4b-80f8-ed798dcdf47d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "38a2ac51-d5be-54ed-b565-78bd1a3a67e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-36371" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Given that the point $P(2,-3,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=6(x-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,-3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-3) & =6(2-1)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,-3) & ={f}_{x}(2,-3)(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6x-12 \\\\ z & =6x-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,-3,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-3 \\\\ z=6x-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=-3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=6x-9$ (or $6x-z-9=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨6,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨6,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-6\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-6⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-6⟩=⟨1,0,6⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6da13587-3d3f-5470-a50e-7c5d0a75e2cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322002} {:answer/id #uuid "10da0c28-49aa-51b3-81e1-2d18d2f02d2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-12\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322003} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c0ea80-ede6-524b-9322-8123deb65dfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-4\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322004} {:answer/id #uuid "dc87c32f-2c60-5e05-a849-fd01a25f9b42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=4x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322005} {:answer/id #uuid "519b4415-c57e-53b7-9b0e-92b9be6c80bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=-4y+12\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4c0ea80-ede6-524b-9322-8123deb65dfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-4\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322004} :answer-field/id #uuid "3954d628-4d5d-527a-90e5-ef106121e032" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34072283-dfb3-5acf-99b2-73755015acf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-36441" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=0.5.$ Given that the point $P(0.5,2,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=y+2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\frac{1}{x}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=0.5.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0.5,2,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(0.5,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)} & =2+2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡((0.5)(2))+\\frac{1}{0.5} \\\\  & =2+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0.5,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-4 & =4(y-2) \\\\ z & =4y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0.5,2,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5eddbac7-927b-5213-85df-660169204d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322009} {:answer/id #uuid "44dc2923-43e6-5ba8-ba14-60d522a61684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322010} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b89cf8-7d79-56d5-8db2-3531bec087a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322011} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d599d8-7400-5fcb-84b4-4a9d937b2604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322012} {:answer/id #uuid "74f41e2a-620e-5649-9430-5390670de978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44dc2923-43e6-5ba8-ba14-60d522a61684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322010} :answer-field/id #uuid "17da8771-82c2-5ba7-b963-1f0b8cb7839e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cde8878-d1f7-5e10-aeab-7b3b6336ac1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-64968" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $0$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\sqrt{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+\\frac{\\pi }{4})$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=0$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =\\sqrt{2}(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e671d02-3be9-599f-8498-1f0470cf8072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322016} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc02da0-c8a4-5fd0-aace-6fae8e8f22f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3i+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322017} {:answer/id #uuid "88a9c96f-283f-59b6-8c97-cb2d710d23f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322018} {:answer/id #uuid "d72b8b3f-ed70-5049-83ed-e150e1e0ecfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322019} {:answer/id #uuid "ae641c86-c39b-5f04-9d92-346f62d73bd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e671d02-3be9-599f-8498-1f0470cf8072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322016} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1303ce5-14a0-59bc-a030-ea7ee71a4f4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a3ebb85-3220-5af9-9101-50c80b1e42cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-64971" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,1,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2(x+y)-2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =2(1+1)-2=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,1) & ={f}_{x}(1,1)(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2x-2 \\\\ z & =2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2x-1$ (or $2x-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨2,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-2\\,k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-2\\,k)=i+2\\,k$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c30d2a9-e439-5868-bf6a-f0943eccad48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322023} {:answer/id #uuid "f64cf730-beaf-5858-a45c-16cd6bc80fb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322024} {:answer/id #uuid "7361735c-97e0-5615-b0c6-3793c9609d0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322025} {:answer/id #uuid "03c59a88-c6f7-5794-88ea-8e1dca5385fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322026} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b3a63c-6270-52af-92fe-374dadfaa588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03c59a88-c6f7-5794-88ea-8e1dca5385fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322026} :answer-field/id #uuid "53f6c136-16f6-5d6c-9327-383bceb91c49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96ad954b-f367-5bd0-b4a5-cf923aa1850c" :question/math-academy-id "q-71198" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $1$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=3{x}^{2}y-6x{y}^{2}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=1$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,1) & =3(2)^{2}(1)-6(2)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =12-12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6626448e-5b4f-5f98-8a20-7682838c8c0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322030} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f50bb7-8a2a-5602-8722-ed0ffb4f477f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322031} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa8f47b-37f9-57d6-b89b-788e69c98aaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322032} {:answer/id #uuid "11905ccd-6edb-5a46-b398-0390aea63059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=-x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322033} {:answer/id #uuid "1687ea78-571c-5420-b775-22dadb21cbac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6f50bb7-8a2a-5602-8722-ed0ffb4f477f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322031} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5255667-5bb3-563c-b8ce-809e544e6d69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76f63137-b52c-5f1c-a959-b140c362941f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71218" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0,1,2)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)} & =(0)^{2}+(1)^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}(x-0) \\\\ z-2 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf0840cc-8f40-554d-aa27-2ab328a01c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-6,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322037} {:answer/id #uuid "991daaff-2d46-56b2-9cb6-3ae09a927011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322038} {:answer/id #uuid "ea12e558-b041-5760-8e95-5223f775f3a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322039} {:answer/id #uuid "81541cd4-92b4-580d-94a8-5fc9d7efa355" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322040} {:answer/id #uuid "029a2e0e-0f4e-51ca-a7f3-34f2594d5f9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,6,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "991daaff-2d46-56b2-9cb6-3ae09a927011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322038} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8da1d4-0854-5f96-a089-243dfb234ab5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "daf286ba-b2e7-5950-80df-28aebe5b694f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71220" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,-6,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{x(y+x)}}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,-6).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,-6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,-6) & =\\frac{-6-(-2)}{\\sqrt{(-2)(-6+(-2))}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,-6) & ={f}_{y}(-2,-6)(y-(-6)) \\\\ z-1 & =-1(y+6) \\\\ z-1 & =-y-6 \\\\ z & =-y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,-6,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-5$ (or $y+z+5=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,-1,1⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3412a480-0907-594d-975b-67c1d5a0181e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322044} {:answer/id #uuid "e513b108-d128-5817-8929-cdc0a6e85a63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322045} {:answer/id #uuid "3fc2261e-3cfa-5b95-91ca-7c836dd00b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322046} {:answer/id #uuid "632bb2b6-7e80-5acf-a561-337f21f6ce9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322047} {:answer/id #uuid "c482fcda-47fb-577a-af30-3864401afb96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fc2261e-3cfa-5b95-91ca-7c836dd00b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322046} :answer-field/id #uuid "821bc836-4412-5373-8984-9c9b1100abd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "23da9fa3-3fe5-5812-be11-6d830c793b0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-71221" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(-1,1,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3{e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|xy|$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-1,1) & =3{e}^{-1+1}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(-1)\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,1) & ={f}_{x}(-1,1)(x-(-1)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =3(x+1) \\\\ z+1 & =3x+3 \\\\ z & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,1,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3x+2$ (or $3x-z+2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-3\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-3⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-3⟩=⟨1,0,3⟩$ is also parallel to it."}]}
